    {
      "id": "p0077-b003",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Hiragana and katakana are both syllabaries, which means every character is one syllable. You can write any and every Japanese word with hiragana or katakana alone. Kanji is a more complicated writing system that Japan imported from China long ago. Kanji is complex, follows multiple pronunciation rules, contains thousands of characters, and takes years to master. All three systems are used side-by-side in normal, everyday Japanese writing.",
        "raw": "Hiragana and katakana are both syllabaries, which means every character\nis one syllable. You can write any and every Japanese word with hiragana\nor katakana alone. Kanji is a more complicated writing system that\nJapan imported from China long ago. Kanji is complex, follows multiple\npronunciation rules, contains thousands of characters, and takes years\nto master. All three systems are used side-by-side in normal, everyday\nJapanese writing.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.11948141,
          0.15077143,
          0.67778102,
          0.26533571
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.11815746,
          0.15005714,
          0.67910497,
          0.26640714
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          4,
          5,
          6,
          7,
          8,
          9,
          10
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "平假名与片假名都属于音节文字，也就是说，每个字符表示一个音节。任何日语词，都可以只用平假名或只用片假名写出来。汉字则是日本很久以前从中国引进的、更复杂的文字系统：读法规则多、字数达数千，需要多年才能掌握。日常书面日语将三套文字并用。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
      },
      "annotations": [],
      "layout": {
